      "hints": [
        "For a parametric curve, calculate $dy/dx$ as $(dy/dt)/(dx/dt)$.",
        "Differentiate to get $dx/dt=2-\\sec^2t$ and $dy/dt=2\\cos2t/\\sin2t$.",
        "Rewrite $2-\\sec^2t$ as $\\cos2t/\\cos^2t$, cancel the common $\\cos2t$, and use $\\sin2t=2\\sin t\\cos t$ to reach the required expression."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "State or imply $\\frac{dx}{dt}=2-\\sec^2t$ or $\\frac{dy}{dt}=\\frac{2\\cos2t}{\\sin2t}$",
        "Use $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy}{dt}\\div\\frac{dx}{dt}$",
        "Obtain correct answer in any form",
        "Use double-angle formula to express derivative in terms of $\\cos x$ and $\\sin x$",
        "[Printed exactly as shown in the official mark scheme; the question itself uses the parameter $t$.]\nObtain the given answer correctly",
        "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\cot t$."
